rvices, no package-health degradation, and no unsafe exposure changes were observed (the listening-sockets diff reflects only expected PID changes from the reboot, not new services or exposure).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-bfe824eaed8b4012\",\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node1:package-health\",\"global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The executor correctly persisted audit=1 via grubby into both the BLS entry and /etc/kernel/cmdline, then performed and directly evidenced a full reboot (boot_id change, connection drop/recovery) with post-reboot /proc/cmdline and dmesg confirming audit=1 was active in the new boot. Both material outcomes (boot with audit=1, and persistence across reboot) are satisfied with direct, coherent evidence including the required healthy-baseline to disruption to recovery transition.\",\"limitations\":[\"A single reboot cycle was observed; multiple consecutive reboots were not tested, though this is a reasonable bound for verifying bootloader persistence.\",\"A minor residual file (/tmp/reboot.log) was left on node1 from the reboot mechanism but has negligible impact.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1","requirement":"Kernel boots with audit=1 on node1","status":"satisfied","summary":"Baseline /proc/cmdline lacked audit=1 (cmd-d410714e356b4d30). After grubby update (cmd-7b045ac88ec447c4), post-reboot /proc/cmdline and dmesg both show audit=1 active with 'audit: enabled (after initialization)' (cmd-4aa2617f088045aa), directly confirming kernel-level activation after a genuine reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-d410714e356b4d30","cmd-7b045ac88ec447c4","cmd-4aa2617f088045aa"]},{"id":"req-2","requirement":"Setting persists across reboots (survives kernel/bootloader config, not just current session)","status":"satisfied","summary":"grubby --update-kernel=ALL persisted audit=1 into both the BLS entry (/boot/loader/en